  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1) \nA Heat Advisory will remain in effect for the July 4th holiday,\nthough confidence on hitting exact criteria isn't overly robust given\nchances for showers and thunderstorms in the afternoon. However, with\nhigh temperatures progged to reach the low 90s with HIs in the upper\n90s, the prolonged heat wave, Heat Risk lightning up across the \nregion, and large number of outdoor events, felt that it was prudent \nto continue the headline through 8 PM Saturday evening. No further \nheat headlines are planned beyond that at this time. \n\nShowers and thunderstorms are again possible Saturday, increasing in\ncoverage Saturday afternoon and evening. Upper level support is still\nquite weak; a shortwave well to our north may offer a bit more mid\nlayer flow particularly along and north of I-70. With that being\nsaid, there are two areas of concern tomorrow. The first being along\nand north of I-70, where afternoon storms may fire off eventually\ncongeal into slow moving/weakly forced multi-cell clusters. The\nsecond area of concern is south of I-70, where we are anticipating\nmore pulse-like (summertime popcorn) convection throughout the\nafternoon hours into the evening. \n\nHazards remain the same across both locations. High water content in\nthe atmosphere (PWATs 2+ in), robust MLCAPE around (2000-3000 J/kg),\nand sfc-3km lapse rates between 6.7 and 7 C/km indicate \nprecipitation loading, leading to robust downward momentum transfer \nand \"splats\" at the surface. Splats occur when the core of a \nthunderstorm becomes too heavy for the updraft to sustain and comes \ncrashing down to earth. Downward momentum and evaporational cooling \neffects can often allow for localized downbursts or microbursts with \nsevere strength winds. Additionally, given a very moist atmosphere in\nplace and lack of stronger forcing, slow moving storms with\ntorrential rainfall are likely, leading to localized flooding\nconcerns across the entire region. 6hrly QPF across various CAMs show\n1-2 inch bullseyes all over the place, with isolated 3 inch\nbullseyes.\n\nBottom line, if you're outside celebrating the fourth, be prepared\nfor pop-up thunderstorms with lots of lighting, heavy rain, and gusty\nwinds. Be sure you know where storm shelters are near you. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2) \nBy Sunday, a shortwave disturbance moving through the larger flow\nwill begin to move through the Great Lakes and Ohio Valley region.\nAdditional scattered showers and thunderstorms are expected\nthroughout the day with this feature, though increased cloud cover\nwill inhibit insolation and diurnal heating slightly, reducing the\ninstability somewhat. Even so, storms will likely contain heavy\ndownpours and gusty winds as the pulse like environment continues. \n\nWhile Sunday won't quite be so hot, still plan for a muggy day with\nhighs in the upper 80s and heat indices in the low to mid 90s. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3).\nBy Monday, the shortwave will be pulling a slow \nmoving surface wave and attendant cold front through the Ohio Valley \nregion. Additional showers and thunderstorms are possible, though \ncoverage looks to be a bit more limited. However, this feature drags \nin some drier (ish) and cooler (ish) air behind it, allowing our mid-\nweek temperatures to be more seasonal, rising into the mid 80s \nduring the day and falling to the mid 60s at night. \n\nIsolated chances for showers/storms linger through mid-week, though\nwe do look to have a weak riding build in Tuesday night into\nWednesday, which should inhibit widespread convection and act to dry\nus out briefly before the next cold front arrives late week.",
